#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157113 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/id #uuid "78635415-4c41-5fd4-8758-ed522da90e01" :question/math-academy-id "e-7918" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n1. $(0,0)$\n2. $(1,1)$\n3. $(-4,4)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{(0, 0)} &= (y + x)^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{(0, 0)} \\\\ = (0 + 0)^{3} \\\\ = 0✓ \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}\\end{vmatrix}_{(1, 1)}|= (y + x)^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{(1, 1)} \\\\ = (1 + 1)^{3} \\\\ = 8 \\\\ \\ne 0\\cdot \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}\\end{vmatrix}_{(-4, 4)}|= (y + x)^{3} \\mid _{(-4, 4)} \\\\\n&= (4 + (-4))^{3} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157117 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2e9d5626-bb05-5382-91f4-efa0235b09be" :question/math-academy-id "e-7923" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}$.\n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4y - e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4y) - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\ninto the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4(4y - e^{3x}) - 3e^{3x} \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 16y - 7e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\mid _{(0, 1)} &= (16y - 7e^{3x}) \\mid _{(0, 1)} \\\\\n&= 16(1) - 7e^{3(0)} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "ce6ca955-28f1-5912-bd36-dc6ef7be1241" :question/math-academy-id "e-7924" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y$.\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n3. $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0)$, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{(2, 0)} &= (x^{2} - 4 - y) \\mid _{(2, 0)} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} - 4 - (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} - 4 - y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(4) - \\frac{d}{dx}(y) \\\\\n&= 2x - \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n&= 2x - (x^{2} - 4 - y) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} + 2x + 4 + y \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\mid _{(2, 0)} &= (-x^{2} + 2x + 4 + y) \\mid _{(2, 0)} \\\\\n&= - (2)^{2} + 2(2) + 4 + (0) \\\\\n&= 4 \\\\\n&> 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0$,\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$.\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0)$. So, according to the second derivative test, since\n$\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} \\right|_{(2,0)} > 0$,\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e2fd80-9df5-5a3d-a5b0-b1d5eedfeb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156749} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe9616a-1215-5c05-86d7-d819eb888ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156750} {:answer/id #uuid "1b14361b-3b5b-50d8-a274-a353e692828b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156751} {:answer/id #uuid "45cd04e0-4e5a-5dad-b945-b8b246168cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156752} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4e327d-41af-5ec5-93ae-e1fdf9d65ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b4e327d-41af-5ec5-93ae-e1fdf9d65ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156753} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1060dd-4804-5e1f-bd2b-fa1d02eeaa40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156748}] :question/id #uuid "79579563-ab18-517d-b26e-0f91d0999c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-89414" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $y^{′} - y = x^{3} + 3x^{2}$. Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n1. $(-1,-2)$\n2. $(1, 1)$\n3. $(1,-2)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfbed954-287d-59d1-b84c-dcfd937bfac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156755} {:answer/id #uuid "c058f0f0-1fd4-5290-991a-d6035a0b7b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156756} {:answer/id #uuid "33cd120f-c5b2-59ac-af6a-a7d167f77a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156757} {:answer/id #uuid "78974707-6add-5c74-b3a0-b47cb7306b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156758} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7bd5c4-8b3a-50a6-b988-51bf37cd1b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c058f0f0-1fd4-5290-991a-d6035a0b7b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156756} :answer-field/id #uuid "2045fe2e-1aaf-5d54-8506-8247c8ef9dce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156754}] :question/id #uuid "0658c406-65bc-5e75-acc3-6aa4bce27134" :question/math-academy-id "q-89424" :question/problem "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2y + x^{2}$.\n\nGiven that the point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}$ at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f87fb809-b768-536b-a673-297acd18ed8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156827} {:answer/id #uuid "017d2055-3314-53eb-9fdc-3623baa73581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156828} {:answer/id #uuid "435f9c30-e730-553d-8c5f-76f63acfda89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156829} {:answer/id #uuid "49d149b4-349e-5756-b4f5-d1494143593f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156830} {:answer/id #uuid "620ec92d-6935-5875-88b1-3229e3c5bbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "620ec92d-6935-5875-88b1-3229e3c5bbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156831} :answer-field/id #uuid "00673a43-405f-526f-9ba6-48f14156a58e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156826}] :question/id #uuid "0ad3120d-6b06-5341-b227-fa4c2d83974e" :question/math-academy-id "q-89891" :question/problem "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = ye^{x}$.\n\nGiven that the point $(0, e)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(0, e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(0, e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n3. $(0, e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$"}]}
